            "An <strong>arterial line</strong> gives a continuous beat-to-beat pressure trace and painless blood sampling, and is indicated in any patient on a vasoactive infusion, in severe respiratory failure needing frequent gases, in the hypertensive emergency and during major surgery. The radial artery is the usual site (after confirming ulnar collateral flow), then the femoral, brachial or dorsalis pedis; in infants the umbilical artery. The set-up is a pressurised bag of 0.9 per cent saline (not heparinised in most units now) at 300 mmHg driving a flush of about 3 mL/h through a transducer connected to the monitor. The transducer must be <strong>levelled</strong> at the phlebostatic axis — the fourth intercostal space in the mid-axillary line, the level of the right atrium — and re-levelled whenever the bed height or the patient's position changes, because every 10 cm of height error changes the reading by about 7.5 mmHg; and <strong>zeroed</strong> to atmosphere (three-way tap open to air, 'zero' pressed) at the start of each shift and whenever the trace is doubted. The non-invasive cuff pressure is compared with the line at set-up and once a shift; a wide discrepancy means one of them is wrong.",
            "Read the <strong>waveform</strong>, not only the numbers. A normal trace has a sharp upstroke, a dicrotic notch (aortic valve closure) and a smooth decline. An <em>over-damped</em> trace is flattened with a lost notch and under-reads the systolic — air bubbles, a clot, a kink, a loose connection or a soft flush bag; an <em>under-damped</em> trace has sharp overshooting spikes that over-read the systolic — long tubing or too many taps; the MAP is the least affected by damping, which is another reason to titrate to it. The square-wave test (a fast flush that should produce one or two oscillations before the trace returns) checks the system. Hazards: haemorrhage from a disconnection — the line and all connections must be visible, Luer-locked and alarmed with a low-pressure limit; accidental intra-arterial injection — every arterial line is labelled in red and no drug is ever given through it; distal ischaemia — check the hand's colour, warmth and capillary refill every hour and remove the line if it is threatened; infection — insert and dress aseptically and remove it as soon as it is no longer needed; and a false reading from a misplaced transducer that leads to an unneeded vasopressor increase or a missed hypotension.",
