            "Ischaemic heart disease is rising fast in Ghana with hypertension, diabetes, obesity and smoking, and the cardiovascular course (NGP-CVN-101) covered the disease; this lesson is about the unit. An <strong>acute coronary syndrome</strong> is a plaque rupture with thrombus: complete occlusion gives an <strong>ST-elevation myocardial infarction</strong> (STEMI — ST elevation of 1 mm or more in two contiguous limb leads or 2 mm in chest leads, or a new left bundle branch block), partial occlusion gives a <strong>non-ST-elevation infarction</strong> (ST depression or T inversion with a raised troponin) or <strong>unstable angina</strong> (the same without troponin rise). The twelve-lead ECG within ten minutes of arrival is the diagnosis; the troponin, which rises over three to six hours and is repeated at three to six hours, confirms injury but a single early normal value excludes nothing. The first-hour care is the sequence in the diagram: sit up, continuous monitoring with a defibrillator at hand because ventricular fibrillation kills in the first hours, oxygen only if the saturation is below 90 to 94 per cent (routine oxygen worsens infarct size), aspirin 300 mg chewed, a second antiplatelet (clopidogrel 300 to 600 mg or ticagrelor 180 mg) as the cardiologist directs, sublingual then intravenous glyceryl trinitrate for ongoing pain if the systolic pressure allows and the infarct is not right-ventricular, intravenous morphine 2.5 to 5 mg titrated with an antiemetic, and an anticoagulant (enoxaparin 1 mg/kg or unfractionated heparin) — then the reperfusion decision.",
            "For STEMI the goal is an open artery within <strong>120 minutes</strong> of first medical contact. <strong>Primary percutaneous coronary intervention</strong> is the preferred method where a catheter laboratory can achieve it in that time — in Ghana this means a handful of centres in Accra and Kumasi — and the nurse's role is to make the transfer happen fast with the patient monitored, cannulated, loaded with antiplatelets and accompanied. Where it cannot, <strong>fibrinolysis</strong> within 12 hours of symptom onset (best within 3 hours, with a door-to-needle time of 30 minutes) with streptokinase 1.5 million units over 30 to 60 minutes, or alteplase or tenecteplase weight-based, is the treatment, followed by transfer for angiography within 24 hours where possible. The <strong>absolute contraindications</strong> — previous intracranial haemorrhage, ischaemic stroke within 6 months, brain tumour or arteriovenous malformation, active bleeding, major trauma or surgery within 3 weeks, suspected aortic dissection, gastrointestinal bleeding within a month — and the relative ones (uncontrolled hypertension above 180/110, anticoagulation, pregnancy, recent prolonged resuscitation, non-compressible punctures) are checked by the nurse on a written checklist before the drug is drawn up. During and after fibrinolysis: continuous ECG for the reperfusion arrhythmias that signal success (accelerated idioventricular rhythm, transient bradycardia) and the ventricular arrhythmias that need treatment; blood pressure every 15 minutes for the hypotension of streptokinase (slow or pause the infusion, legs up, fluid); watch for the allergic reaction; neurological observations every hour for the 1 per cent risk of intracranial haemorrhage — any new headache, confusion or weakness stops the infusion and calls the doctor; no intramuscular injections, no arterial punctures, minimal venepuncture, pressure on every site; and a record that the patient has had streptokinase, because antibodies make a second dose from four days to at least a year later ineffective and dangerous — alteplase or tenecteplase must be used instead.",
