            "A <strong>hypertensive emergency</strong> is a severely raised blood pressure — usually above 180/120 mmHg — <em>with</em> acute damage to a target organ: hypertensive encephalopathy (headache, confusion, seizures, papilloedema), intracerebral or subarachnoid haemorrhage, acute ischaemic stroke, acute coronary syndrome, acute pulmonary oedema, aortic dissection, acute kidney injury with haematuria, or severe pre-eclampsia and eclampsia. The same pressure without organ damage is hypertensive urgency and is treated with oral drugs over days; admitting it to the ICU and dropping the pressure fast causes strokes. In the true emergency the pressure is lowered by intravenous infusion with an arterial line, by <strong>no more than 20 to 25 per cent of the MAP in the first hour</strong>, then towards 160/100 over the next two to six hours, then to normal over 24 to 48 hours, because the brain, heart and kidneys of a chronically hypertensive patient have reset their autoregulation and a 'normal' pressure is, for them, hypoperfusion. Three exceptions: <strong>aortic dissection</strong>, where the systolic is brought below 120 and the heart rate below 60 within twenty minutes with a beta-blocker first (esmolol or labetalol) and a vasodilator second; <strong>acute ischaemic stroke</strong>, where pressure is lowered only if above 220/120 (or above 185/110 before thrombolysis) because the penumbra depends on it; and <strong>severe pre-eclampsia</strong>, where the target is 140–150/90–100 with labetalol, hydralazine or oral nifedipine and magnesium sulphate for the brain (lesson 7.1). The drugs are labetalol (first choice in most, and in pregnancy), glyceryl trinitrate (with pulmonary oedema or coronary syndrome), hydralazine (pregnancy), nicardipine where available, and never sublingual nifedipine capsules, which cause uncontrolled falls. Nursing: arterial line, neurological observations with the blood pressure, hourly urine output, the headache and vision asked about each hour, and the trigger — missed drugs, cocaine or amphetamines, a phaeochromocytoma, renal artery stenosis, the pregnancy — sought.",
