            "<strong>Pulmonary embolism</strong> presents as sudden breathlessness, pleuritic pain, haemoptysis, tachycardia, hypoxia with clear lungs and a low PaCO₂, with a source in the leg, the pelvis after surgery or childbirth, a long journey, a malignancy, the combined pill, sickle cell disease or a previous clot. <strong>Massive</strong> embolism — hypotension, syncope, a strained right ventricle on echocardiography, a raised troponin — is an obstructive shock with a mortality above 30 per cent and is treated with <strong>systemic thrombolysis</strong> (alteplase 100 mg over two hours, or 50 mg as a bolus in cardiac arrest with resuscitation continued for 60 to 90 minutes) unless absolutely contraindicated, followed by heparin; <strong>submassive</strong> embolism (right ventricular strain with a normal pressure) is anticoagulated and watched closely; all others are anticoagulated with low-molecular-weight heparin (enoxaparin 1 mg/kg twice daily) or an unfractionated heparin infusion if thrombolysis may follow or the kidneys are failing, then warfarin or a direct oral anticoagulant for three to six months or longer. Nursing: oxygen to saturation 94 to 98, an upright position, cautious fluid only — a dilated right ventricle is worsened by litres, and noradrenaline is preferred for hypotension — a CT pulmonary angiogram or, where unavailable, bedside echocardiography and leg ultrasound, no intramuscular injections once thrombolysis is contemplated, and the prevention that should have happened: mechanical and pharmacological thromboprophylaxis for every ICU patient without a contraindication, reviewed daily.",
